<div class="usm-card usm-section">
    <div class="usm-card-header">
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M8.5 8.5l7 7M15.5 8.5l-7 7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <h3>Command blacklist</h3>
    </div>
    <p>One regular expression per line, checked using ordinary "appears anywhere in the command"
       matching (unlike the whitelist above, which requires a full match) &mdash; so a pattern
       here still catches a dangerous fragment tucked after a <code>;</code> or
       <code>&amp;&amp;</code> in an otherwise-allowed command. A match here rejects the command
       even if the whitelist would have allowed it. This is <em>your</em> deny-list, layered on
       top of &mdash; never a replacement for &mdash; the hard-coded safety blocklist described
       below, which always applies no matter what and can't be edited.</p>
    <textarea class="usm-list" name="commandBlacklist" id="usmBlacklistText"><?= htmlspecialchars($blacklistText) ?></textarea>
    <p>
        <button type="button" class="usm-btn" onclick="usmLoadExample('usmBlacklistText', 'usmBlacklistExample')">Load example blacklist</button>
        <em>A starting-point set of explicit denials for things worth blocking even under an
        otherwise permissive whitelist &mdash; review and adjust, then Save settings to apply.</em>
    </p>
    <pre id="usmBlacklistExample" style="display:none">\breboot\b
\bpoweroff\b
\bshutdown\b
\bdocker\s+(rm|stop|kill|restart|rmi)\b
\bdocker\s+system\s+prune\b
\biptables\b
\bpasswd\b
\bssh-keygen\b</pre>

    <p style="margin-top:16px;"><strong>What's in the hard-coded safety blocklist?</strong> Checked
       first, before your whitelist or blacklist. It can't be edited, and "Allow all commands" does
       not bypass it &mdash; only the separate "Disable the hard-coded safety blocklist" switch near
       the top turns it off entirely (don't, unless you mean it). It targets operations that would
       destroy the array, the boot device, or the filesystem wholesale:</p>
    <ul style="margin:6px 0 12px 18px;padding:0;">
        <li>Direct writes to a raw block device (<code>/dev/sd*</code>, <code>/dev/nvme*</code>,
            <code>/dev/md*</code>, <code>/dev/hd*</code>, <code>/dev/xvd*</code>) via
            <code>dd</code>, shell redirection, <code>tee</code>, <code>cp</code>,
            <code>install</code>, <code>rsync</code>, or <code>dcfldd</code></li>
        <li><code>mkfs</code>, <code>wipefs</code>, <code>shred</code>, and
            <code>blkdiscard</code> against a block device</li>
        <li>Destructive Unraid array commands (<code>mdcmd stop</code>,
            <code>mdcmd nocheck</code>, <code>mdcmd clear</code>)</li>
        <li>Recursively deleting <code>/</code> or everything under it &mdash;
            <code>rm -rf /</code> and equivalents, including long-form flags like
            <code>rm --recursive --force /</code> in any order</li>
        <li>Classic shell fork bombs</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>It's a backstop for catastrophic mistakes, not a substitute for a narrow whitelist —
       plenty of things that aren't wholesale destruction (like overwriting an important config
       file, or a container's data) are still worth keeping out of your whitelist or explicitly
       denying above.</em></p>
